nswer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e7d887d-e0e7-5390-878c-560d04e1e11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379013} :answer-field/id #uuid "576ab971-a207-5967-8d37-5db8a4c60c7d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8a204fe-0678-5217-8fd6-73d54f821b1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-175909" :question/problem "$$\n{{\\sqrt{2}\\text{ is irrational}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{1-2=1}_{⏟}}_{B}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $A$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $B$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $A∨B$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $A$ is true, and $B$ is false. Therefore, the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\sqrt{2}\\text{ is irrational}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{1-2=1}_{⏟}}_{B} & \\,\\,≡\\,\\text{true}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ab93ca-16ca-5e57-835f-036b59f25d63" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Truth Values of Compound Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f4e9e86-8284-5124-a5d8-2986c678430f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051966} {:answer/id #uuid "351e3b6e-328b-51b3-a3b4-654ed5082185" :answer/typ